<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio López de Santa Anna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio López de Santa Anna was a Mexican general and politician who dominated the country's affairs from the 1820s to the 1850s and served as president several times. He helped overthrow the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>, presided over the centralist republic, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> in 1836 and commanded during the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. His last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> sold land to the United States in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> before he was driven out in 1855.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna.jpg" alt="Antonio López de Santa Anna" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meade Brothers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Lopez_de_Santa_Anna_c1853.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Santa Anna was born in Xalapa, Veracruz, on 21 February 1794 into a respected criollo family. His father was a lawyer, and his mother supported his wish for a military career against his father's wish that he be a shopkeeper. Through her friendship with the intendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> he received a military appointment on 6 July 1810, though under age. He fought for the Spanish crown against the insurgency for over a decade, switching to the insurgents in 1821. His origins on the coast gave him immunity to yellow fever, an advantage over officers from elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>, ruler of the new Mexican Empire, gave him command of Veracruz and then removed him; in December 1822 Santa Anna rebelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalupe-victoria">Guadalupe Victoria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-casa-mata">Plan of Casa Mata</a> of 1 February 1823 called for a republic. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> he forced the Spanish general Isidro Barradas to surrender on 11 September 1829, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s last attempt to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and styled himself the Victor of Tampico.</p><p>He was elected president on 1 April 1833 but left government to Vice President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-gomez-farias">Valentín Gómez Farías</a>, whose anticlerical reforms he ended by dissolving Congress on 12 June 1834. In 1835 he repealed the liberal Constitution of 1824; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> declared independence on 2 March 1836, and his expedition to recover it ended in disaster.</p><p>He lost part of a leg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastry-war">Pastry War</a> of 1838 and used this to re-enter politics. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> he took the presidency in 1846 and fought on to the end; one historian says that more than any other single person it was Santa Anna who denied Polk's dream of a short war. Invited back by conservatives, he was elected president on 17 March 1853 and declared himself dictator for life as His Most Serene Highness. He ceded territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> in 1854, and the Plan of Ayutla removed him in 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1855 to 1874 Santa Anna lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Colombia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> he tried unsuccessfully to sell chicle for carriage wheels, though he introduced chewing gum to the United States. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-lerdo-de-tejada">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</a> allowed him to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1874, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 21 June 1876. Historians call the thirty years after independence the Age of Santa Anna and have called him an uncrowned monarch. He is often remembered in Mexico as a vendepatria, a traitor to the fatherland, because of the territory lost to the United States during his rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio López de Santa Anna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When <b>Antonio López de Santa Anna</b>'s regime adopted the Siete Leyes, abolishing the authority of the states, and began enforcing the anti-slavery laws, the settlers objected to mandatory Catholicism and feared worse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 12 November 1853 President <b>Antonio López de Santa Anna</b> announced a competition for a national anthem, with a prize for the best poem of patriotic ideals.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-nacional-mexicano">Mexican National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mexican–American War, United States troops under Winfield Scott outflanked the larger Mexican army of <b>Antonio López de Santa Anna</b> at Cerro Gordo and drove it from a strong defensive position.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1835 a conservative congress backed by <b>Antonio López de Santa Anna</b> abolished the federal system of the First Mexican Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Antonio López de Santa Anna</b> rose at Veracruz in December 1822, and the Plan of Casa Mata in 1823 won over the army.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Vicente Guerrero abolished slavery in 1829, the year <b>Santa Anna</b> defeated a Spanish reconquest attempt at Tampico.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-republic">First Mexican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xalapa, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.53,-96.92&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Xalapa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1932 1440 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonio López de Santa Anna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2382 1710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2382 1710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.53,-96.92&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
